from write_records import add,N
add('eg',1,'Explore motivational change without selecting only action tools','Map several forms of motivational change before choosing a single persistence intervention.','A motivation inquiry initially suggests routines, incentives, and schedules; I have not yet compared whether interest, meaning, social setting, and role can be separate targets.', '''Space: constructed ways an inquiry’s motivational character could change. In scope: perceived choice, intrinsic interest, relation to goals, challenge, role, feedback, social reciprocity, and physical/digital setup. Out of scope: diagnosis, private biography, claims of measured human change. Valuable finding: a distinct target that changes a generated experiment or comparison. Waste: eight names for the same habit reminder.

Knowledge state: dimensions partly known; choose a systematic sweep. Proposed effort allocation follows the original 70/30 exploration/exploitation split as a planning heuristic, not an empirically optimal ratio. Actual pass ledger contains eight exploratory passes and three later use comparisons; this is an operation count, not a timed 70/30 measurement.

| Pass | Region explored | Concrete finding and surprise | Rating / next |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Choice | Same reading task can be self-selected or assigned; these differ without changing content | Interesting; adjacent role |
| 2 | Intrinsic interest | A question can attract attention even if its answer is useless | Interesting; do not demand payoff |
| 3 | Meaning | Tedious checking can serve a valued shared artifact without becoming enjoyable | Significant; separate reasons |
| 4 | Challenge | Too-easy and too-hard are different candidate obstacles; no personal level known | Minor; needs actual task evidence |
| 5 | Role | Observer, proposer, and critic can find different aspects worth attending to | Interesting; compare role prompts |
| 6 | Feedback | A score and a specific discrepancy supply different information | Significant; draft alternatives |
| 7 | Reciprocity | An answer from another person can change what is interesting next | Interesting; human response absent |
| 8 | Setup | Ready materials lower entry steps but do not by definition create interest | Minor; boundary already established |

Synthesis: motivational reason, experienced interest, task entry friction, and role-specific attention are separate constructs. The surprising distinction is meaningful-but-unenjoyable work versus interesting-but-instrumentally-useless work; the former is not failed interest and the latter is not failed usefulness by definition. This is a conceptual comparison, not a claim about the user.

Three exploitation comparisons executed: draft a reading invitation emphasizing voluntary question choice; draft a meticulous review invitation emphasizing contribution to a shared artifact; draft a critic role invitation emphasizing finding a consequential counterexample. Each targets a different reason. A generic “stay motivated” reminder is not selected because it does not specify which variable changes.

Open questions: which reason a real person endorses; whether any draft changes voluntary engagement; whether the same framing changes meaning while reducing curiosity. Next direction: go deeper into the meaningful/unpleasant versus interesting/useless contrast. No pivot away from broad mind change is adopted.''','The exploration now covers motivational reasons and roles beyond persistence routines, but its drafts have not changed a real person’s motivation.','Three distinct candidate interventions are specified; their comparative effects remain untested.','UNRESOLVED','Content gains target diversity. Organization separates finding ratings from outcome evidence and does not let a “significant” exploratory rating become a KEEP.','Compare real preference among the three invitations; follow the role-specific attention thread; test a setting where no motivation change is desired.',N+' Eight exploration passes, explicit discovery criteria, three exploitation comparisons, synthesis and next-direction choice.')
add('eg',2,'Explore forms of reciprocal disagreement','Find whether productive interaction can preserve disagreement instead of using agreement as the sole discovery criterion.','An exploration of cooperation initially makes convergence on a shared answer look like the clearest sign of progress.', '''Space: constructed interactions in which two people investigate the same question with different priorities. Boundaries: voluntary discussion, explicit information, no covert influence. Value criterion fixed before passes: a finding changes what each party can jointly do while preserving actual disagreement. Waste criterion: merely renaming compromise.

Strategy: expand from known disagreement dimensions—facts, interpretation, values, timing, roles, risk, ownership, and standards. Seventy/thirty remains the source’s proposed resource split, not a measured efficacy claim. Eight discovery passes and three later applications are recorded.

| Pass | Region | Finding | Rating / next |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Facts | Different denominators can produce apparent disagreement without rival goals | Interesting; resolve arithmetic |
| 2 | Interpretation | Same silence can mean access missing or assent; evidence discriminates | Minor; reuse social map |
| 3 | Values | Artist and archivist can agree on replicas while disagreeing about originals | Significant; preserve scope |
| 4 | Timing | One person needs a decision now, another wants a revisitable record | Interesting; split commitment horizon |
| 5 | Role | Facilitator can record reasons without deciding which aesthetic is best | Interesting; role separation |
| 6 | Risk | One party can run a reversible trial while another withholds endorsement | Significant; cooperation without assent |
| 7 | Ownership | A shared object does not imply either party may irreversibly alter it | Interesting; retain authority |
| 8 | Standard | Consensus on a procedure does not settle truth of its output | Significant; separate acceptance claims |

Synthesis: convergence is one outcome, alongside clearer conflict, bounded trial, informed abstention, and agreed division of authority. Biggest surprise: a trial can proceed with explicit dissent if the relevant authority and exposure permit it; unanimity is not built into the definition of cooperation. This is a case possibility, not a recommendation for every group.

Three later applications executed: a mock decision record says “trial permitted, conclusion disputed”; a facilitation prompt asks for the strongest unresolved objection after selecting a reversible test; a handoff says who may change which object. These are actual generated artifacts using the explored distinctions. They do not show that a group will accept them.

Open questions: which disagreements are genuine constraints rather than misunderstandings; whether dissenters have a real option not to bear risk; whether an apparent reversible trial imposes irreversible time or reputation costs. Next direction: deepen the authority/risk boundary, not force an agreement outcome.

Certificate: the constructed permitted trial with retained dissent is a counterexample to “cooperation requires shared conclusion.” It does not imply that any group should proceed over objections. The specific permission and exposure premises remain load-bearing.''','The candidate outcome space now includes bounded cooperation with explicit dissent. Real cooperative effects remain untested.','Generated decision records preserve disagreement while naming what is authorized to proceed.','UNRESOLVED','Content expands outcome types without claiming group agreement. Organization records conclusion, permission, and exposure separately.','Test a case where dissent includes a binding right; compare trial permission with full endorsement; seek actual participant feedback on the record.',N+' Eight passes, three applications, explicit criteria, synthesis, and a retained authority/risk crux.')
add('hab',1,'A small curiosity ritual remains an untested human habit','Design a repeatable noticing practice without calling its written plan a formed habit.','A small daily question after an existing routine seems a plausible way to invite curiosity; the actor’s actual cues and rewards are not available.', '''Constructed actor: a learner who has voluntarily chosen to notice one unfamiliar feature in an ordinary object after opening a notebook. Build mode. Behavior: write one concrete question about the object, then optionally investigate. Current frequency stipulated as occasional; desired frequency is on chosen learning days, not every calendar day.

Cue: opening the notebook for an already chosen session. Reliability is conditional on that session happening; no reliable daily routine is invented. Minimum routine: notice one visible relation and write one question, under two minutes. Full version: follow one question for ten minutes and record a changed description. Scaling: only after the actor finds the minimum usable; no automatic escalation is assumed.

Immediate reward design: the actor can choose a pleasing question to carry forward. Tracking: a small mark indicates the question was written, not that curiosity increased. Identity link proposed as “a person who notices concrete differences”; acceptance unknown and optional. The source’s claims that a particular reward or identity link is universally necessary/most powerful are not assumed proven.

Eight obstacles and specific mitigations: no object nearby → use notebook edge; no interesting feature → allow a plain observation; session skipped → no fictitious failure mark; question too broad → name a visible relation; perfectionism → one rough line; routine feels imposed → stop/reselect freely; tracking crowds out looking → mark after; setup materials absent → use the existing page. Recovery after a missed chosen session is an invitation to resume at the next one, not a moral rule.

Environment: visible blank “question” line; one tool already open; no extra app setup; optional pairing with a pleasant object; feedback is the actual question. Measurement: chosen opportunities, completed minimums, optional continuation, and self-reported interest would be recorded separately. Review after two weeks if a person undertakes it; adjustment if the routine repeatedly displaces the wanted inquiry. No habit-established threshold is declared from a streak.

Habit formation data show substantial variation in automaticity trajectories; one fixed day count is not justified for this practice. [Lally and colleagues, primary study](https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/ejsp.674)

Later application: generated first question for the cup case is “Which part of the handle supports weight, and which part merely creates room?” The minimum behavior is executed once in this text. No human repeated behavior, automaticity, or sustained interest is observed.

Certificate: one completed gateway action establishes one occurrence, not a habit. The plan is concrete; the behavioral mechanism and future effect remain untested.''','The practice has a precise cue, minimum, and measurement plan; no formed habit or increased interest is inferred.','One candidate noticing action is available and demonstrated once.','UNRESOLVED','Content distinguishes opportunity, occurrence, continuation, and interest. Organization keeps tracking marks tied to exactly what happened rather than an identity claim.','Observe voluntary use across actual opportunities; compare no-tracking and simple-tracking variants; let the actor reject the identity framing.',N+' Seven design steps, eight obstacle cases, separate behavior/interest metrics, and one actual generated question.')
add('hab',2,'Replace automatic agreement with a truthful pause','Design a break-habit plan for a constructed actor who says yes before knowing whether a request fits their commitments.','A prepared refusal phrase seems a plausible replacement for automatic agreement, but it may replace indiscriminate yes with indiscriminate no.', '''Constructed input: an actor reports saying yes to optional group tasks before inspecting time and scope, then withdrawing. Break mode. Exact behavior to interrupt: immediate acceptance of an undefined task. Current pattern is stipulated; real user history is not used. Desired behavior: acknowledge the request, identify its scope and deadline, then make an honest choice.

Cue: a new optional task request addressed to the actor. Reliability: situational, not daily. Current routine: “Sure” before task definition. Underlying-need hypotheses: maintain connection, avoid silence, help the group, appear reliable. They are not elicited intrinsic values. Replacement: “What is the deliverable and deadline? I can decide once those are clear.” This can still end in yes.

Minimum: one scope question. Full: compare the defined task with actual commitments, answer yes/no/alternative, and record the accepted boundary. Immediate reward candidate: relief from an undefined promise, unobserved. Tracking mark means “defined before accepted,” not “became assertive.” Identity link is proposed as reliable contributor, not a fixed self-definition.

Eight obstacles: public pressure → ask the same brief factual question; genuine emergency → state what can be done now; vague answer → no acceptance implied; request already clear → choose directly; fear of seeming unhelpful → offer a bounded contribution if wanted; repeated request → reference the prior boundary; existing promise → honor or renegotiate explicitly; a quick easy task → no artificial delay needed. Recovery: if an undefined yes occurred, clarify promptly rather than preserve an image of perfect streak.

Environment: a visible “deliverable / deadline / owner” line beside incoming tasks; no extra barrier for clearly understood requests. Make automatic yes less immediate by drafting the scope question first, not by imposing punishment. Accountability: voluntary self-review after several actual requests; social partner only if chosen.

Measurement: proportion of optional requests defined before acceptance; later withdrawals; whether accepted work is completed. Interest in collaboration and interpersonal acceptance remain different outcomes. Review after five real opportunities, not elapsed days. Adjustment trigger: the replacement becomes an excuse never to help or creates unnecessary questioning for already clear tasks.

Later application: constructed request “Can you help with the exhibition?” gets a scope question; “Can you label these three replica boxes by 4?” gets a direct bounded acceptance in the generated dialogue. This demonstrates a conditional replacement, not a changed human habit.''','The replacement is a scope-sensitive choice rather than an automatic refusal. The recurring human effect is untested.','The constructed dialogue preserves helpfulness and honest commitment boundaries.','UNRESOLVED','Content retains the original cooperative need as a hypothesis while avoiding a rigid opposite routine. Organization keys the replacement to undefined requests only.','Observe real requests of differing clarity; compare acceptance accuracy before and after; test whether the scope question itself becomes unnecessary friction.',N+' Seven original steps, eight obstacles, build/break-specific environment design, and two contrasting generated responses.')
add('hf',1,'Two minutes can open inquiry without standing in for inquiry','Design a gateway routine whose completion mark does not exaggerate the substantive practice achieved.','A two-minute daily practice is an appealing starting point for a constructed learner; its tracking scheme might overstate what the gateway actually accomplishes.', '''Build habit: on a voluntarily chosen study day, open the saved question and write one concrete example. Motivation in the case: become able to investigate independently, while retaining curiosity. Done for gateway = one example written; done for full practice = compare that example with a counterexample. Desired frequency is chosen sessions, not every day by default.

Four-law design: obvious cue is the already-open study page; attractive feature is the learner’s own selected question; easy response is one example; satisfying feedback is seeing a concrete object for inquiry. These are design choices, not proven causal laws for this actor. Environment: the question and prior example are visible, irrelevant notifications absent in the proposed setup, materials prepared, no unsolicited social accountability.

Two-minute initial version executed in text: question “Does sharing a goal require sharing reasons?” Example: artist and archivist both choose a replica trial, one for creative freedom and one to protect originals. This starts inquiry; it does not yet test the universal. The full version adds a counterexample to necessity: their different reasons coexist with the shared trial choice.

Tracking fields: opportunity occurred; gateway done; full comparison done; optional continuation; interest report if a human supplies it. The last field is not inferred from the first four. Self-accountability uses a written review after five opportunities; no financial commitment or public performance promise is added.

Eight obstacles/monitoring variations: page unavailable → record question locally; example absent → note a concrete uncertainty without checking full completion; two minutes exceeded → choose whether to continue; interest gone → revise/stop rather than fake progress; missed day → resume next chosen opportunity; full practice not done → retain gateway-only mark; social demand appears → preserve voluntary scope; tracking becomes goal → inspect actual comparisons.

Monitoring: one gateway and one full comparison occur in this document; no daily human log, weekly review, or automaticity trend exists. The source’s “make it impossible to fail” is treated as design aspiration, not literal fact. Lapses and automaticity require actual observations. [Lally and colleagues, primary habit-formation study](https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/ejsp.674)

Later application: a generated practice record marks “gateway yes / full inquiry no” after merely opening a difficult claim. This preserves the larger goal; the transfer is a measurement distinction already explicit in the program’s evidence rules, not an additional KEEP.''','The gateway is performed and separately marked from the fuller practice; no recurring habit has been established.','The written tracking scheme avoids treating preparation as substantive completion.','UNRESOLVED','Content provides a usable small entry and a distinct full behavior. Organization has two completion fields rather than one inflated streak.','Observe a learner’s actual opportunities; compare examples-first and counterexamples-first entry; test whether tracking full practice discourages voluntary entry.',N+' Seven steps, four-law design, eight obstacle/monitoring variations, a gateway execution and a separate fuller comparison.')
add('hf',2,'A streak cannot be protected at every cost','Test the original routine’s absolute streak instruction against the program’s broader value commitments.','Protecting a small voluntary routine usually seems compatible with reliable practice; the phrase “at all costs” adds an unlimited priority that has not been justified.', '''Constructed break-habit target: a learner automatically records a completion mark even when the intended inquiry did not occur, because the visible streak is about to break. Underlying motivation: retain an appearance of consistency. This is a stipulated scenario, not a claim about the user.

Four laws for breaking: cue—hide the streak total while the session is underway; craving—distinguish actual inquiry from a satisfying mark; response—require naming the concrete comparison before a full-completion mark; reward—let the record show partial or missed without resetting the value of previous work. No shame, payment, or punishment is proposed. Environment: record fields are “attempted,” “gateway,” and “comparison,” with previous substantive outputs accessible.

Small replacement: write one truthful state, including “not attempted.” This is record repair, not the desired inquiry itself. Tracking: report what happened, do not redefine full inquiry to guarantee a streak. Accountability: voluntary self-inspection; social pressure is not added.

Eight costs that the absolute rule would permit by its words: false completion; deleting counterevidence to finish; violating an agreed commitment; continuing after the actor has chosen to stop; replacing the original goal with a trivial token; hiding missed opportunities; penalizing legitimate context changes; suppressing exploration that does not fit the streak. Each conflicts with an explicit relevant value in the constructed design or the supplied program.

Concrete execution: a mock log has day 1 full comparison, day 2 gateway only, day 3 skipped by choice. The honest record remains [full, gateway, skipped], not [full, full, full]. The first full comparison remains available; no claim that a missed interval erases learning is made. In the cited habit study a missed opportunity did not materially disrupt the modeled formation process, but that observation is not a universal guarantee for all habits. [Lally and colleagues](https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/ejsp.674)

Monitor/review: the mock log tests representational honesty only. Actual daily execution, weekly review, and future automaticity are absent. Replacement routine can itself become avoidance, so a later review distinguishes truthful nonperformance from beneficial practice.

Subsequent application: a fictional writing log records “opened document, no paragraph written.” It is not credited as paragraph completion. The absolute streak instruction is rejected, while small viable starts remain usable.

Certificate: an instruction that permits false completion to preserve a streak conflicts with truthful evidence of broad beneficial change. One such permitted cost defeats “at all costs.” Strongest contrary branch: “at all costs” is figurative encouragement; that is a weaker interpretation and can remain a separate candidate, but does not rescue the literal universal.''','I reject unlimited streak preservation; it cannot override truthful recording, voluntary choice, or the underlying practice.','The constructed logs retain partial and skipped states without inventing full work.','REJECT','Content separates the source’s literal absolute from a weaker motivational metaphor. Organization preserves actual states and the value of previous substantive work.','Test an optional streak with honest partial states; compare no-streak tracking; examine when a small gateway is itself the entire desired behavior.',N+' Seven formation/breaking steps, eight explicit cost countercases, a mock three-opportunity log and a later record.')
